---
import CalculatorLayout from '../../layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import NetToGrossCalculator from '../../components/calculators/NetToGrossCalculator';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "When is a net-to-gross calculation useful?",
    answer:
      "Whenever the constraint is expressed in take-home terms: a rent to cover, a mortgage affordability test, or a salary negotiation where you know what you need in the account each month. Working forward from gross answers a different question, and converting mentally between the two is unreliable.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does the province change the required gross?",
    answer:
      "Considerably. Federal tax, CPP and EI are the same everywhere, so the entire difference comes from provincial tax, and on a middle income the spread between the lightest and heaviest province can exceed several thousand dollars of gross for the same take-home pay.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is the result exact?",
    answer:
      "It is accurate to a few dollars for a standard salaried position. Because the relationship between gross and net is not linear, the calculation works by narrowing in rather than applying a formula. Personal credits beyond the basic amount, a benefit in kind, or a mid-year province change all shift it.",
  },

  {
    question: 'How does the net-to-gross calculator work?',
    answer: 'The calculator uses binary search to find the gross salary that results in your desired net (take-home) pay. It factors in federal tax, provincial tax, CPP/QPP, EI, and any additional premiums like the Ontario Health Premium, then iterates until the resulting net pay matches your target within $1.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why do I need to specify my province?',
    answer: 'Provincial tax rates vary significantly across Canada, from as low as 4% in Nunavut to over 25% in Quebec. A target net salary of $50,000 requires a different gross salary in each province. Quebec residents also pay QPP instead of CPP and contribute to QPIP, which changes the calculation further.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I use this to negotiate my salary?',
    answer: 'Yes. If you know the take-home pay you need each month, this calculator helps you determine the minimum gross salary to ask for. Remember that employer benefits (RRSP matching, health insurance, etc.) also have value beyond your cash salary. It is also worth calculating what the role costs the employer, since that figure, rather than your gross, is the budget the offer is drawn from.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does the calculator account for RRSP contributions?',
    answer: 'The basic net-to-gross calculation does not include RRSP contributions. If you make RRSP contributions, your required gross may be lower because RRSPs reduce your taxable income. Use the main salary calculator with the RRSP field for that scenario. Employer matching, where it exists, changes the arithmetic further: a matched contribution is compensation you receive without paying tax on it in the year it is earned.',
  },
];
import PayrollContext from '../../components/PayrollContext.astro';
import OfficialSources from '../../components/OfficialSources.astro';
---

<CalculatorLayout
  slug="/net-to-gross"
  title="Net to Gross Calculator"
  metaTitle="Net to Gross Salary Calculator Canada 2026 | Reverse Tax"
  metaDescription="Find the gross salary you need to earn to achieve your desired take-home pay in Canada. Reverse tax calculator for all 13 provinces, updated for 2026."
  h1="Net to Gross Salary Calculator Canada 2026"
  intro="Enter your desired take-home pay and find out what gross salary you need to earn. Updated with 2026 CRA tax rules for all 13 provinces and territories."
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <NetToGrossCalculator client:idle />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <section class="mt-12 prose prose-gray dark:prose-invert max-w-none">
      <h2>How to Calculate Gross Salary from Net Pay</h2>
      <p>
        Converting net salary to gross isn't as simple as reversing a percentage, because Canada's tax system is progressive, meaning different portions of your income are taxed at different rates. Multiple deductions (federal tax, provincial tax, CPP, EI) each have their own thresholds and caps, making a simple formula impossible.
      </p>
      <p>
        This calculator uses an iterative approach: it tests different gross salaries, calculates the net result for each, and narrows down until it finds the exact gross that produces your target net pay. The result accounts for all federal and provincial deductions applicable in 2026.
      </p>

      <h2>Why Gross Salary Varies by Province</h2>
      <p>
        A target net salary of $50,000 requires different gross salaries depending on your province because each province has different tax brackets. For example, achieving $50,000 net might require approximately $66,000 gross in Alberta but over $72,000 in Quebec, due to Quebec's higher provincial rates and QPP contributions.
      </p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/individuals/frequently-asked-questions-individuals/canadian-income-tax-rates-individuals-current-previous-years.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CRA: 2026 Tax Rates</a></li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
  <PayrollContext />
  <OfficialSources />
</CalculatorLayout>
